# `stands as` and `serves as` dress up `is` as a role (`the index
# serves as a lookup table` becomes `the index is a lookup table`).
# Each needs a determiner after `as`, because the other readings
# compare or qualify (`stands as tall as`, `stands as written`, `serves
# as many requests as`, `serves as root`, `serves as-is`).
expr main [(stands as DET), (serves as DET)]
let message "Write is."
let description "Flags stands as and serves as before a noun phrase, where is carries the meaning."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The cache stands in front of the database."
allows "The tower stands as tall as the building beside it."
allows "The regulation stands as written until the appeal is decided."
allows "The record stands as of this morning."
allows "The server serves as many requests as the socket buffer allows."
allows "The daemon serves as root, which is the reason for the seccomp filter."
allows "The device serves as-is with no warranty."
allows "The daemon serves as it always has, on port 80."
